  "notes": "The other New York analog retro-soul producer-arranger. `confidence` is **low** — the role, the instruments and the label context are sourced, the parameters are inference from the records. The entry separates him from the Daptone template explicitly: his harmony is \"minor-mode cinematic soul — `i–iv–V` and `i–VII–VI–V` descents, half-diminished colour, and long chromatic bass lines; **harmonically darker and more arranged than the Daptone template**\", which is why the two families it names carry a raised `susOrDimProb` and a lower triad weight than the Daptone preset, and why the bass carries a chromatic passing-tone set. Drums are \"a dry, damped, break-style kit tracked to tape — kick on 1 and the offbeat of 2, snare cracked on 2&4, tambourine and bongos, single-stroke fills, and a deliberately narrow-bandwidth drum tone. **The kit is designed to be sampled.**\" So `secondaryAnchor` is 2&, the ghost map is a single position (a break has no ghost bed), the hat is an 8th at 0.14, and the percussion is tambourine, bongo and conga. He is a saxophonist-arranger and the melody is his horn line rather than a vocal: \"instrumental tracks with no vocal are a large part of the output, and the horn or string theme *is* the melody\", with \"tenor/baritone sax section, flute, vibraphone, harpsichord, string quartet, and heavily-tremoloed guitar\" as the palette. ⚠ Every drum number and every melody figure here is INFERRED. ⚠ His two progression families are the same ones the research gives Aloe Blacc, whose records he is credited on; the two models are separated by tempo, kit and extension colour rather than by an invented difference in the harmony. ⚠ The \"instrumental cover of a rap record\" format he is known for is a repertoire choice, not a parameter, and is not encoded.",
